description:
The objective of this procurement is to implement a fit-for-purpose, trauma informed, culturally safe wellbeing support service available to:
provide pre-engagement, on the day psychological first aid/grounding, and limited post-engagement debriefs and referrals;
reduce the risk of distress, re-traumatisation, and safety incidents;
support safe participation for diverse groups, including people with service experience in the Australian Defence Force, people with disability, Aboriginal and Torress Strait Islander peoples, culturally and linguistically diverse (CALD) particpants, LGBTQIA+ participants, young people, and others with specific needs; and
integrate with engagement operations while safeguarding confidentiality, privacy, and the independence of the Defence and Veterans' Service Commission.

The Invitation to provide Day Procedure Centre Services to the Veteran Community seeks to ensure that the veteran and Defence Force communities, and their treating doctors, continue to have wide access to high quality stand-alone Day Procedure Centre services.
Eligible providers are invited to submit an Invitation Response Proforma and apply to contract with DVA through a new, simplified arrangement. The key features of the new arrangements are:
an overall objective of maximising the numbers of participating providers, to achieve wide access to DPCs for the veteran community;
there is no formal comparison of responses against each other;
providers are required to submit their fee tables and acknowledge their compliance with the terms and conditions of this Invitation;
providers declare their ability to comply with DVA’s terms and conditions of the DPC Services Agreement;
providers can submit a response and apply to enter into the DPC Services Agreement at any time while this Invitation is available on AusTender;
the Agreement will continue until either party ends the arrangements; and
there is a standard DPC Services Agreement with negotiation only on fees to ensure value for money.

The Customer invites Queensland-based research providers, including both university and non-university organisations, to deliver a 12-month research project examining trauma exposure and psychological wellbeing within the Queensland emergency dispatcher workforce including Emergency Medical Dispatchers (Queensland Ambulance Service), Police Communications Operators (Queensland Police Service) and Fire Communications Officers (Queensland Fire Department).

The research will:

i.	quantify and analyse trends in the psychological wellbeing, trauma exposure, workforce participation, absenteeism, retention and other relevant workforce indicators among emergency dispatchers;

ii.	identify organisational, leadership, supervisory and workforce practices that contribute to positive psychological wellbeing, workforce retention and job satisfaction;

iii.	examine factors that support successful transition into emergency dispatcher roles, including recruitment, onboarding, training and early workforce experiences;

iv.	explore the lived-living experience of emergency dispatchers, including factors associated with psychological distress and positive workforce outcomes;

v.	identify similarities and differences across participating agencies and operational centres, including opportunities for cross-agency learning and shared improvement strategies; and

vi.	highlight opportunities for system improvements that strengthen psychological safety, workforce sustainability and trauma-informed organisational responses within the Queensland emergency dispatcher context.

vendorPanelDetails.background:
Suppliers are invited to propose a fit for purpose research design that can deliver robust and policy-relevant insights. Core activities include:

•	Mixed-methods research design incorporating both quantitative and qualitative components;

•	Quantitative analysis of available workforce and administrative datasets (subject to approval), including the analysis of:

 o	psychological wellbeing indicators

 o	trauma exposure indicators

 o	workforce retention and turnover

 o	absenteeism and leave utilisation

 o	workers compensation and psychological injury data (where available)

 o	recruitment and onboarding outcomes

 o	job satisfaction and workforce engagement indicators

 o	other relevant psychosocial and workforce metrics;

•	Qualitative data collection, including interviews and/or focus groups with emergency dispatchers and relevant stakeholders;

•	Comparative analysis with relevant national and international literature to contextualise findings;

•	Identification of key risk factors, protective factors, and systemic gaps in current workplace supports;

•	Review and synthesis of relevant existing agency research, evaluations and workforce wellbeing initiatives to build upon current evidence and avoid duplication of efforts;

•	Identification and analysis of organisational practices, supports and interventions currently perceived to be effective, including supervision models, peer support, wellbeing initiatives, leadership approaches and workplace adjustments;

•	Examination of factors contributing to successful transition from training into operational emergency dispatcher roles;

•	Comparative analysis across participating agencies and operational centres to identify transferable lessons and examples of good practice;

•	Consideration of potential organisational and economic benefits associated with improved workforce wellbeing, retention and participation, including development of evidence to inform future investment decisions where feasible;

•	Development of evidence-informed, trauma-informed system improvement recommendations; and

•	Clear translation of findings into practical, actionable strategies for participating agencies.

